chuckcar <chuckcar@nil.org> writes:
> rose.josef@googlemail.com wrote in

> > Experimenting under a different name shows that the keys I hit were
> > probably Ctrl-Z, but I'm not sure about this.

Like others said, fg is the command to return to the game if you used
ctrl-z.

> > still learning. I've tried typing "recover" into the command line, to
> > be told that the recover program isn't installed - however, I'm not
> > sure if this is the right program for nethack.

It is recover, but it probably isn't in your path. You would need to go to
the playground directory (/usr/games/lib/nethackdir in the default setup)
and use 
./recover alock
if the set of level files for that game (probably the most recent files
with names matching *lock* pattern in that directory) are alock.0,
alock.1, alock.2 and so on.
And you probably need to run it as root.
But it is possible that you don't have the recover utility, and in this
case you probably wouldn't need it anyway.

> shutdown. For future reference if it happens again, all you have to do
> is type: exit. You suspended the game to enter a bash shell. You can

Use exit if you pressed ! when you got the prompt, but not after ctrl-z.
After ctrl-z, fg is the right command like others said.

> disable this when you compile it. It *might* also be in the "O"ptions in
> which case you could disable it in your ~/.nethackrc or NETHACKOPTIONS
> line in /etc/profile, but I'm not sure. 

There is no runtime option to disable ! or ctrl-z, only the compile time
option SHELL normally #defined in unixconf.h to enable or disable the !
command. I don't think there is anything to disable ctrl-z, an least not
something that could be done just by commenting out some #define.

